      <h1>About Peaks and Puddles</h1>

      <h2>From Swiss Peaks to Scottish Puddles</h2>
      <p>
       There is a magic moment when a child realises that deep, squelchy mud isn't something to avoid, it's the entire point.</p>

      <p>Growing up in Switzerland and spending years working in the outdoor industry, being outside in all conditions was just life. You learn the hard way how gear performs: why cotton betrays you, how cheap waterproofs fail and what real alpine-grade durability looks like.</p>
     
      <p>Now, with a 4-year-old at forest nursery in Scotland, I’m constantly testing those old industry certainties against daily muddy trails.</p>
      <p>
       Good kit changes everything. When children are warm and dry, bad weather stops being a barrier and becomes an invitation, extending the fun long after everyone else has gone inside. We're still learning every day out there, one peak and puddle at a time but this is the resource I wish I'd had to help other families cut through the marketing noise and stay out longer.</p>

      <h2>What Peaks and Puddles Is All About</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Peaks and Puddles</strong> started as a place to share honest, technical gear advice for parents navigating forest school, muddy dog walks and winter adventures and it is growing into a wider hub for getting outside with young kids.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Gear That Survives Real Weather:</strong> From merino base layers and bib-and-brace dungarees to insulated wellies and mittens that actually stay on, we translate technical outdoor specs into plain English.</li>
        <li><strong>No-Nonsense Trade-Offs:</strong> Expensive isn't always better for fast-growing kids. If a £25 wipe-clean pair of dungarees beats a £90 technical shell for daily nursery mud, I will tell you straight.</li>
        <li><strong>Family Adventures, Activities & Holidays:</strong> Because the right kit is only half the story, you will also find practical guides to kid-friendly outdoor activities, seasonal micro-adventures, and active family holidays—plus our own curated <strong>Peaks and Puddles</strong> outdoor merch down the line.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How We Illustrate & Protect Our Family's Privacy</h2>
      <p>
        All gear insights and outdoor tips on this site come from hands-on family life in Scotland, years of mountain sports, and close fabric-spec comparisons. To protect my child's privacy online, I never publish identifying photos of my son—alongside licensed editorial stock imagery used for illustration, any real-world gear shots you see here are strictly neck-down outfit checks, muddy boots by the back door, and flat-lays of the kit itself.
      </p>

      <h2>Transparency, Partnerships & Affiliate Disclosure</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Peaks and Puddles</strong> is an independent, mum-run platform built to keep families warm, dry, and outdoors. To support the site and keep our guides free to read, we fund our work in a few transparent ways:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Affiliate Links:</strong> Some links on our pages are affiliate links. If you click through to a retailer and buy something, we may earn a small commission at no extra cost to you. <em>As an Amazon Associate I earn from qualifying purchases.</em> We also partner with vetted outdoor and family retailers via networks such as Awin.</li>
        <li><strong>Selected Brand Partnerships:</strong> We occasionally work with carefully vetted, genuinely good outdoor brands on sponsored placements or featured gear spotlights. Every partner brand is held to the same real-world weather standards we use for our own family, and any paid placement is always clearly labelled.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Get in Touch</h2>
      <p>
        Have a question about sizing winter layers, want to share a favourite muddy trail, or interested in a brand partnership? Drop me an email anytime at <a href="mailto:hello@peaksandpuddles.co.uk">hello@peaksandpuddles.co.uk</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        See you out in the mud,<br />
        <strong>Founder & Mum, Peaks and Puddles</strong>
      </p>
